WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 40.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.300
It's May 16th, 2026.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.400
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.340
Let's dive in.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.300
So imagine someone could make a video that looks exactly

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.580
like you saying things you never said.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.300
That fake video could spread on social media

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.740
before you even know it exists.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.560
YouTube just rolled out a free tool that

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.780
lets every adult creator fight back against those deep fakes.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.560
The system asks you to record a short selfie-style video

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.560
of your face once.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.060
YouTube's AI then treats that recording like a fingerprint

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.380
of how you look and move.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.980
From then on, it watches new uploads

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.220
and sends you an alert if it spots something

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.800
that matches your likeness but is not really you.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.540
Think of it like handing the platform a photo of your house key

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.000
so it can spot any copies someone tries to slip into a door.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.420
Deep fakes have already shown up in school projects

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.280
and everyday online harassment.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.800
This tool gives you an early warning

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.820
instead of finding out from friends after the video spreads.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.340
The feature is rolling out now in the creator tool section

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:05.980
of the YouTube app or website.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.980
You just need your phone camera

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.980
and a couple of minutes to record the scan.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.300
No coding or special gear required.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.300
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.440
the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.580
Today's concept is called mixture of experts

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.020
and the new EMO model uses it in a clever way.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.860
Picture a big group project at school

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.620
where you do not make every person do every job.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.480
One teammate handles research, another builds the slides

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.780
and someone else practices the presentation.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.020
The AI model works the same way inside.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.640
It keeps different expert parts that each specialize

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.020
in one kind of task,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.000
like answering science questions or writing stories.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.020
When a new question arrives,

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.660
the model quickly picks only the experts

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.420
that match that question instead of waking up the whole team.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.320
The EMO model takes this further

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.740
by training those experts on whole topics

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.760
rather than tiny word patterns.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.360
That means you can turn off most experts

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.940
and still get almost the same quality answers.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.060
The result is an AI that runs on phones or laptops

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.720
without huge amounts of memory

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.600
because it is not dragging every expert along for every task.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.620
So next time someone says mixture of experts,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.500
picture that smart study group

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.140
that only calls in the right teammates.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.080
And that is basically how the new approach works,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.920
not so mysterious after all.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.080
Now let's look at some cool stuff you can try yourself.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.360
First up is a free Mac app called Osaurus

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.880
that puts both local and cloud AI in one place.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.320
It lets you run models that live on your own computer

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.580
alongside bigger models that run online.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.840
Your files stay private on your device when you want

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.340
yet you can still reach stronger cloud models for bigger jobs.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.680
Perfect for students working on homework

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.620
or creative projects while keeping control of their data.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.980
Download Osaurus from the Mac app store,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.160
open a saved PDF on your computer

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.580
and ask it to summarize the file first.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.720
Then switch to a cloud model

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.380
for a more creative writing task

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.220
and watch how the answers change.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.080
No terminal commands are complicated setup.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.900
Just pick a model from the menu and start chatting.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.480
Next, runway is making AI video generations simple enough

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.180
for anyone.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.260
Their tool turns a text description into a short video clip.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.620
If you enjoy making TikToks or YouTube shorts,

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.280
this could let you create visuals

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.940
without filming everything yourself.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.260
Head to the runway site, sign up for the free tier

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.940
and type a prompt like a cat skateboarding

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.420
through a neon city at night.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.700
Try different styles and notice how the motion looks

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:43.980
in the five-second clip.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.600
Experimenting like this helps you see what the AI handles

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.180
well and where it still struggles.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.620
Time for a couple of quick bits.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.700
A lab called Andon Labs built four radio stations

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.200
run entirely by AI models like Cloud,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.860
Chat GPT, Gemini, and Grock.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.380
The models pick music, write scripts,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.840
and host the shows with zero human help,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.580
which creates some weird and off-topic moments.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.380
It shows how far agent style AI has come

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.340
while also highlighting what happens

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.960
when no one steers the output.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.160
Google updated its search rules

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.040
so anyone trying to trick its AI overviews

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.880
gets treated like traditional spam.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.320
Stuffing content with hidden keywords

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.660
or fake signals will now hurt rankings

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.860
the same way old spam tactics did.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.840
The change should lead to cleaner,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.120
more honest answers at the top of results

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.540
as more people rely on AI summaries.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:37.900
And that's a wrap.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.060
If any of today's stories made you go,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.460
huh, that's cool, go play with it.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.060
Curiosity is how every expert started.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:45.960
See you tomorrow.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.100
And if you'd rather watch, then listen,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.640
find us on YouTube at Nerra Network.

